### Cycloscope Integration Test Collection
### Format: VS Code "REST Client" extension (each ### block is one request).
### To use in Postman instead: File → Import → this file (Postman reads .http/.rest files directly).
###
### Run top to bottom. Start services first: MongoDB → backend → ml/serving → frontend.
### See INTEGRATION_TEST.md in the project root for expected results and pass/fail criteria per step.

@backendUrl = http://localhost:5000/api
@mlUrl = http://localhost:8000
@cycloneId = IO_2026_TEST
@sessionId = test-session-001

### ============================================================
### STEP 0 — ML service reachable directly (sanity check before backend)
### ============================================================

### 0a. ML health check
GET {{mlUrl}}/health

### 0b. ML intensity predict (manual test image — replace with a real base64 IR patch)
# POST {{mlUrl}}/predict/intensity
# Content-Type: application/json
#
# {
#   "cyclone_id": "{{cycloneId}}",
#   "image_base64": "<base64-encoded test IR frame>",
#   "channel": "IR"
# }

### ============================================================
### STEP 1 — Backend health (Mongo connection + process up)
### ============================================================

GET {{backendUrl}}/system/health

### Expected: 200, { "status": "ok" } or equivalent, no Mongo connection errors in backend logs.

### ============================================================
### STEP 2 — Backend can reach the ML service
### ============================================================

GET {{backendUrl}}/system/ml-status

### Expected: 200, indicates "live" (ML reachable) — not "fallback" — assuming ml/serving is running.

### ============================================================
### STEP 3 — Seed a test cyclone before triggering predictions
### ============================================================
### Run this in mongosh (not an HTTP call) against your MONGODB_URI, once, before Step 4:
#
# db.cyclones.insertOne({
#   cycloneId: "IO_2026_TEST",
#   name: "Test Storm",
#   basin: "Bay of Bengal",
#   season: 2026,
#   status: "active",
#   currentCategory: "Cyclonic Storm",
#   currentWindSpeedKmh: 85,
#   currentPressureHpa: 995,
#   lastUpdated: new Date(),
#   currentLocation: { lat: 15.0, lon: 88.0 },
#   source: "live-feed"
# });
#
# db.climatologyprofiles.insertOne({
#   basin: "Bay of Bengal",
#   month: new Date().getMonth() + 1,
#   avgWindSpeedKmh: 90,
#   avgPressureHpa: 990,
#   avgTrackBearingDeg: 315,
#   avgTrackSpeedKmh: 15,
#   sampleSize: 42
# });
### The ClimatologyProfile row is required for Step 5 (fallback path) to return a real estimate
### instead of erroring — without it, fallbackService.js has nothing to look up.

### ============================================================
### STEP 4 — Full live chain: backend -> ML -> Mongo -> readable back
### ============================================================

### 4a. Trigger a refresh (this is what the 6h scheduler calls internally, on demand)
POST {{backendUrl}}/predictions/{{cycloneId}}/refresh

### Expected: 200, body has "source": "ml-model", a "modelVersion", detection/intensity/trackForecast populated.

### 4b. Read it back
GET {{backendUrl}}/predictions/{{cycloneId}}/latest

### Expected: same PredictionResult just written, persisted in Mongo.

### ============================================================
### STEP 5 — Fallback path: stop ml/serving, then repeat Step 4a
### ============================================================
### Manual step: kill the ML service process now, then re-send this request.

POST {{backendUrl}}/predictions/{{cycloneId}}/refresh

### Expected: 200 (NOT an error to the client), body has:
###   "source": "fallback-climatology"
###   "fallbackReason": "ingestion-failure" or "model-failure"
###   wider "uncertaintyRadiusKm" than the live-model response from Step 4a
### Also check: an AlertLog entry was written with the same fallbackReason.
### Restart ml/serving before continuing to Step 6.

### ============================================================
### STEP 6 — Frontend-facing reads (what the dashboard actually calls)
### ============================================================

### 6a. Active systems list (Dashboard screen)
GET {{backendUrl}}/cyclones/active

### 6b. Single cyclone detail (Cyclone Detail View)
GET {{backendUrl}}/cyclones/{{cycloneId}}

### Expected: both return data consistent with Steps 4/5 — confirms the frontend has real
### data to render, not just the backend having it. Load the actual frontend dashboard now
### and confirm: risk badge shows, uncertainty cone renders, and the amber fallback banner
### appeared during Step 5's window (check PredictionResult.source on that specific record).

### ============================================================
### STEP 7 — RAG chatbot end-to-end
### ============================================================

POST {{backendUrl}}/chat/{{sessionId}}/message
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "message": "What's the current status of the test storm near Bay of Bengal?"
}

### Expected: 200, grounded answer referencing the seeded cyclone's data (not a generic/fabricated
### answer), response is tagged for the frontend's "AI-generated, verify with IMD" label.

### 7b. Confirm it persisted
GET {{backendUrl}}/chat/{{sessionId}}/history

### Expected: both the user message and assistant response present, with retrievedContextIds populated.

### ============================================================
### STEP 8 — Authentication & RBAC Operator Profiles
### ============================================================

### 8a. List all certified operator profiles & clearances
GET {{backendUrl}}/auth/operators

### 8b. Authenticate as Operational Meteorologist
POST {{backendUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "meteorologist",
  "password": "password123"
}

### 8c. Authenticate as Cyclone Research Analyst
POST {{backendUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "analyst",
  "password": "password123"
}

### 8d. Quick 1-click login by role
POST {{backendUrl}}/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "role": "coordinator"
}

